ition. It holds no presumption of infallibility or binding authority and must compete on equal evidentiary footing with other ancient versions to explain the original unpointed consonants.\",\"burden_of_proof_rule\":\"The burden of proof is strictly symmetrical for any two admissible vocalizations. Neither the Masoretic pointing, critical editions, nor modern consensus enjoys a presumption of originality. Both candidates must demonstrate their validity from consonants, grammar, context, and prior Scripture.\",\"dependent_witness_rule\":\"Manuscripts, translations, or scribal traditions sharing a single common ancestor or demonstrably copying from one another are counted as a single witness to avoid artificially inflating the numerical weight of a reading.\",\"source_bias_rule\":\"Traditions with known theological motivations (e.g., anti-Christian polemic in later Jewish transmission, or pro-Christian harmonization in later Christian transmission) must be scrutinized. A reading contrary to a source's expected bias is granted higher independent weight, while readings conforming to known biases are strictly evaluated against prior Scripture.\",\"special_context_rules\":[\"Poetic parallelism requires evaluating readings based on structural symmetry, meter, and established word pairs.\",\"Prophetic and typological texts must be evaluated against the covenantal framework of blessings and curses outlined in Deuteronomy before assigning symbolic meaning.\"],\"tie_rule\":\"If, after applying all comparative weights and NT confirmation checks, both readings remain equally plausible explanations of the consonants and context, the evaluator must declare the text UNDERDETERMINED.\",\"anti_ad_hoc_safeguards\":[\"All rules, evidence weights, and hierarchical rankings must be rigidly fixed before the specific disputed word or desired theological outcome is identified.\",\"No new rules, exceptions, or novel lexical definitions may be introduced to rescue a preferred reading that fai